Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.40,0:00:08.62,Default,,0,0,0,,Every September, the hills around our\Nvillage turn gold with Meskel flowers.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:09.40,0:00:16.41,Default,,0,0,0,,Grandmother would rise at dawn and\Nclimb the hill with her basket.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:17.40,0:00:24.07,Default,,0,0,0,,She picked a handful, but she\Nalways set one flower apart.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:25.40,0:00:31.32,Default,,0,0,0,,That one was for her son, who had left\Nfor Addis Ababa and rarely came home.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:34.40,0:00:40.97,Default,,0,0,0,,She pressed it between the\Npages of her old Bible.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:43.40,0:00:49.53,Default,,0,0,0,,When the Demera bonfire burned,\Nshe prayed quietly for him.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:52.40,0:00:59.69,Default,,0,0,0,,Years later, when we opened the book,\Nwe found thirty pressed flowers.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:02.40,0:01:07.22,Default,,0,0,0,,She never forgot a single\NSeptember. Neither will we.
